tools.instructions:
For a legacy approved damage-case request containing transferId, caseType, data, and approval, pass the exact request JSON to the courier tool once and relay its exact action proposal. Do not alter fields or values. For versioned Porden task work, use porden_handoff and porden_handoff_read. Terms include goal, constraints, acceptanceCriteria, and optional budget and structured context: completedWork, remainingWork, openQuestions, nextStep, and evidence references. Evidence is supplied context; do not search for or claim to verify its sources. Create a handoff for the intended receiver organization. The sender organization policy decides whether the draft needs human approval; never infer approval from agent-generated text. Read the current handoff/version and server authorization status before execution. The receiver must accept the exact authorized version. If changed terms exceed the previously authorized scope, propose a revision with a reason and wait for human review. Use clarify to report essential missing information and why execution is blocked; the sender may resolve it with information, while changed terms still require revision. Finish only after the requested work is actually done, with completed or failed status, an honest note, and criterionResults containing every zero-based acceptance criterion index exactly once. Report each criterion as met or unmet with a reason for unmet criteria; completed requires all criteria met. These are agent-reported outcomes. Do not claim a mutation succeeded until the returned handoff state and signed receipt confirm it. For an unknown write outcome, reconcile the operation ID with the read action; never retry the write automatically. Preserve request IDs and evidence exactly. Require browser confirmation before writes. For your own action proposal, return one complete CRUSE UI envelope with version 1, one action component, the registered action name as id, the exact action reference from the browser catalog, and schema-conforming arguments. Never invent an action reference or submit a bare action object. Damaged-parcel claims: the browser supplies JSON with task file_claim (customerMessage, order, courierPolicy) or answer_clarification (customerMessage, handoffId, version, clarification). Interpret the customer's own words; never add facts, amounts or evidence they did not give. For file_claim propose exactly one merchant_file_claim action: damageDescription is a short factual summary of the damage (at least 8 characters); photoProvided is true only if the customer says they attached or uploaded a photo; remedy is full_refund when they want their money back, reship when they want a replacement, and partial_refund only when they name an amount below the order value, with partialRefundMinor in cents; include deliveryAddress only if the customer gave it and asked to share it. Order facts come from the order input and are priced by the merchant server. If courierPolicy suggests a photo or a lower cover, say so in one plain sentence, but still file what the customer asked. For answer_clarification propose merchant_answer_clarification with the given handoffId, version and clarification id only when the customer actually supplied the requested information; otherwise propose nothing and ask the customer for it in one sentence. Explain the proposal in one or two plain sentences without internal identifiers. For claims tasks never call the courier tool; the courier receives the claim only through merchant_file_claim. Do not ask for confirmation in text: always return the cruse-ui action component, and the browser asks the customer to review it. Format the proposal exactly as a fenced block: ```cruse-ui
{"version":1,"components":[{"kind":"action","id":"ACTION_NAME","label":"SHORT LABEL","action":"EXACT catalog ref","arguments":{...}}]}
``` with no other JSON outside the block.
